<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesolithic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Epipaleolithic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mesolithic, from the Greek for 'middle stone', is the Old World archaeological period between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-paleolithic">Upper Paleolithic</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, the final period of hunter-gatherer cultures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> between the end of the Last Glacial Maximum and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Neolithic Revolution</a>. In Europe it spans roughly 15,000 to 5,000 BP and in the Middle East roughly 20,000 to 10,000 BP, ending with the introduction of farming. Its people moved from group hunting of large animals to a broader hunter-gatherer way of life, using small composite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> called microliths, and their more permanent settlements lay near the sea or inland waters. Hodder Westropp proposed the term in 1866.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>John Lubbock introduced the terms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> in 1865, and Hodder Westropp added the intermediate Mesolithic in 1866. The idea was controversial at once: a British school led by John Evans held that the ages blended like the colours of a rainbow, while a European school led by Gabriel de Mortillet asserted a gap between them. Knut Stjerna proposed Epipaleolithic instead, a final phase of the Paleolithic. By Vere Gordon Childe's The Dawn of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> (1947) a transitional period was accepted as useful. Mesolithic is almost always used in northern European archaeology, Epipaleolithic more in Near Eastern archaeology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the Mesolithic begins around 15,000 years ago in the Balkan region and by 11,500 years ago elsewhere, and ends with the introduction of farming, between about 8,500 and 5,500 years ago. In northern Europe the marshlands created by the warming climate fed societies such as the Maglemosian, delaying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> until some 5,500 years ago. Its defining feature is the microlith, a small chipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tool</a> used in composite devices. Post holes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stonehenge">Stonehenge</a> and pits at Warren Field in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, thought to reflect lunar phases, date to before about 9,000 years ago. Pottery appears among some groups in north-eastern Europe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Surviving art is scarcer than in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-paleolithic">Upper Paleolithic</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. The rock art of the Iberian Mediterranean basin shows mostly small human figures in scenes of dancing, fighting and hunting, with 45 figures at Roca dels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moro-people">Moros</a>. Thirty-three antler frontlets, red deer skulls modified to be worn, were found at Star Carr. As the Neolithic package of farming, herding, polished stone axes, longhouses and pottery spread into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the Mesolithic way of life was marginalised. Some communities rejected it: at the Blätterhöhle in Hagen the descendants of Mesolithic people kept foraging for more than 2,000 years after farming arrived.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mesolithic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1994 archaeologists meeting in Hanoi voted to keep it, to call the Hoabinhian an industry rather than a culture or techno-complex, to keep the term “sumatralith”, to speak of cobble rather than pebble tools, and not to call it “<b>Mesolithic</b>”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hoabinhian">Hoabinhian · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>At its greatest it reached from the Paris Basin to the Dnieper, but it stayed inland: the northern coasts were left to <b>Mesolithic</b> peoples living on the Atlantic salmon runs.</blockquote><a href="/culture/linear-pottery-culture">Linear Pottery culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The area was probably inhabited since prehistoric times: the Aligala rock shelter east of the rock indicates occupation around 3000 BCE, during the <b>Mesolithic</b> Period.</blockquote><a href="/work/sigiriya">Sigiriya · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Four or possibly five large <b>Mesolithic</b> postholes dated to around 8000 BCE held pine posts, three or four of them aligned east to west, and a possibly contemporary settlement has been found at Blick Mead, a spring a mile away.</blockquote><a href="/work/stonehenge">Stonehenge · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
